"Money creation in the modern economy | Bank of England – the UK's central bank (https://www.bankofengland.co.uk/quarterly-bulletin/2014/q1/money-creation-in-the-modern-economy)\nciteturn32view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://www.bankofengland.co.uk/quarterly-bulletin/2014/q1/money-creation-in-the-modern-economy\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 64\nL0: 【0†Skip to main content】\nL1: \nL2: Published on 14 March 2014\nL3: ### By Michael McLeay, Amar Radia and Ryland Thomas of the Bank’s Monetary Analysis Directorate.\nL4: This article explains how the majority of money in the modern economy is created by commercial banks making loans. Money creation in practice differs from some popular misconceptions — banks do not act simply as intermediaries, lending out deposits that savers place with them, and nor do they ‘multiply up’ central bank money to create new loans and deposits. The amount of money created in the economy ultimately depends on the monetary policy of the central bank.\nL5: In normal times, this is carried out by setting interest rates. The central bank can also affect the amount of money directly through purchasing assets or ‘quantitative easing’.\nL6: 【1†Money creation in the modern economy】\nL7: \nL8: 【2†Money creation in the modern economy: Long-run data annex】\nL9: \nL10: 【3†Play This short video, filmed in the Bank of England’s gold vaults, explains how money is created in the modern economy video†youtu.be】\nL11: \nL12: [Button: Convert this page to PDF]\nL13: ## Other Quarterly Bulletin 2014 Q1 articles\nL14: \nL15: 【4†Publication // Quarterly Bulletin 14 March 2014 The Court of the Bank of England The Court of the Bank of England】\nL16: \nL17: 【5†Publication // Quarterly Bulletin 14 March 2014 Dealing with a banking crisis: what lessons... Dealing with a banking crisis: what lessons can be learned from Japan’s experience?】\nL18: \nL19: 【6†Publication // Quarterly Bulletin 14 March 2014 M